what I took from Kilimanjaro I learned  the power of my story I'm not trying to  be an inspiration what I try to do is be  an example of like if this is what I can  do what can we all do  [Music]  my name is Spencer West I'm a  motivational speaker and an author I was  born with a genetic disease that  basically caused the muscles in my legs  not to work I had two surgeries the  first one was at the age of two where  they were gonna remove my legs at the  knee the hope was I would use prosthetic  legs to get around that way but it  didn't work then at the age of five they  were removed just below my pelvis so I  could get around better after my  surgeries my parents met with the  doctors and they basically told my  parents I would probably never walk by  myself sit up by myself or be a  functioning member of society I grew up  in a small town called Rock Springs in  the state of Wyoming everyone on our  block we all knew each other we all  played together and what was so  interesting is that they treated me just  like everybody else they like a rode a  skateboard with folks in my neighborhood  I felt like I belonged but it was when I  started school that things started to  change at recess I didn't want to play  sports with the boys so like I was  always playing house with the girls and  like hanging out with them because of  that you know I was called a [ __ ] I  was called queer I was called gay and  auntie's for always hanging out with the  girls and that was really hard because I  was starting to have an idea that maybe  I was gay as well and then when  something like that happens you're  terrified and you don't want to be that  anymore and so you pretend not to be my  first real career was working at a salon  and spa this was an incredible job and I  made some amazing friends there and I  really enjoyed the people that I worked  with but the job didn't fulfill what I  was looking for I didn't feel like I had  a purpose there other than just to make  money  in March of 2008 I went to Kenya with my  friend Reed and his partner Greg and I  saw the work that this organization we  was doing and I remember flying back to  Nairobi we were coming from the Masai  Mara  I was asking you know what would it take  to work for the organization my  facilitator said oh you'd have to move  to Toronto and I said oh okay  after a couple of years at we I'm  speaking all across the world now and  I'm working with students and I'm  working with other people telling that  listen you need to make a difference you  need to create awareness you need to  create a campaign that will either raise  funds or raise awareness about the  things that you care about specifically  I was traveling with her founder Craig  Kielburger and he had just nonchalantly  been like hey have you ever thought  about climbing Kilimanjaro and I was  like no what and a week later I thought  wow  maybe I could I can show you that like  I'm gonna do exactly what I tell them to  do and so I thought about it for a week  and I went back to Craig and I said yes  I want to do this but this is what I  want to do I want to create a campaign  and he basically said go do it I went to  my two buddies their names are David and  Alex and I was like I can't do this by  myself and what if we like have this  really cool adventure as friends we  named our campaign redefined possible  and initially we wanted to raise money  to build schools then that summer East  Africa got hit with one of the largest  droughts they'd seen in over 60 years so  we decided to do clean water instead got  a personal trainer we trained really  hard a lot of people were super excited  and couldn't wait to get involved my  poor mother what's like so terrified and  this is like her worst nightmare I'm  like someone that pushes the limits a  lot for myself but I kept thinking maybe  this is like too far that first day we  definitely panicked a little bit because  it was so much harder than we thought we  had trained that I would do 50% of my  hands and 50% of my wheelchair I knew  for sure that I liked I couldn't do the  whole mountain on my hands your arms are  not meant to be walked on like that so  that was the hope that though I was  gonna do half and half that wasn't  accurate it worked for a little bit and  then we got to the actual part where the  trail started and my wheelchair was too  wide so that okay this first Stacia  I can get out and I can walk a little  bit and then I can rest then I can walk  a little bit and that's what we did but  very quickly we started to realize that  this is how it was going to be there  wasn't a lot of options for me to use my  chair so I either needed to really give  in and walk half the day and then have  my friends carry me or if there were  places I could use my wheelchair they  could push me which I wasn't prepared  for I wanted to do more than that the  other thing that was really hard is that  I had to give up my independence and as  someone with a disability you spend your  entire life fighting for independence  and that was hard and in those moments  there's nothing that you can do but just  accept it the morning of summit it's  dark outside all you can see is this  beautiful blanket of stars it's so cold  it like our water in our water bottles  had head froze  Kilimanjaro isn't a technical climb it's  just weather the altitude affects you  were not the first half  I needed my friends and they were  amazing it physically and emotionally  supporting me and then  the roles reversed and my buddies got  altitude sickness and I didn't I just I  didn't if there's no rhyme or reason  there's no explanation I always make a  joke that it's because I'm so short at  that point they were like it is far too  steep for you to walk on your hands and  for us like carry you in this  contraption and so they tied me in a  blanket on the porters back and  literally carried me that way and I'll  never forget as we were zigzagging we  literally watched night turn in today on  one side it was still dark as the Sun  came up on the other side we finally got  to the point where it's like the last  part before you start walking towards  the top called Gilman's point  Kilimanjaro is a volcano and an active  volcano and so you can see now we have  to walk around the rim which then leads  to the highest point the boys had  prepared some videos for me because they  knew we would need something for this  last push so we had a video from my  family we all just started to walk  together and that's when my other  friends started to get sick  I wasn't feeling altitude sickness but  you feel the altitude every motion takes  all of your efforts if I were just like  jump out of this chair here would be no  problem but on Kilimanjaro if you did  that it would take you a minute to catch  your breath now both of my friends were  so sick that they were throwing up  something as simple as your foot  slipping a little bit on the ice my  friend Alex just would crumble like to  his knees the porters had started to  carry me again because it was too  dangerous for me to walk along the rim  in some spots and then we got to one  point where we could rest again and I  just said you need to put me down  because my buddies were so sick I felt  so guilty I thought I was the one that  was gonna need help and now here they  were suffering because I asked them to  come and I asked him to support me and  now the roles are reversed and they  needed my help the entire climb they  kept saying you know what it's really  inspiring to watch you walk up this  mountain I was like f blah blah but the  minute we stopped I thought what else  can I do honestly it was the first day  ever in my entire life where I wanted  legs that day so I could like carry my  friends like they had carried me and so  what kept playing over in my head is it  so inspiring to watch you walk my  parents have always taught me to focus  on the things that I could do so I  thought well this is all that I know to  do so we literally stood together and I  stood in between them and I said we're  just gonna walk hand over hand and foot  of her foot and as slow as we need to we  walked a little bit more for an Alex  puked walked a little bit more crumbled  picked him up walked a little bit more  and we could see it just  off in the distance there it was there  it was and slowly we just kept walking  and stopping and walking and stopping  and eventually we we made it to the  summit it felt like slow motion we  slowly got there and we just collapsed  into each other's arms and just cried  but it was bittersweet  we like hugged we like took a minute to  just like be in the moment I went to  Kenya to facilitate one of our youth  trips and I remember I got there and my  body just gave out and I got the flu and  I was like out of commission for two  days I remember laying in that tent and  having separation anxiety within you  know a day of each other we got message  each other more like we really miss you  and we all miss each other that's when  it hit me  Wow we did that we did that we made it  to the top and we showed the world that  anything is possible  [Music]  I really want people to recognize is  that we all have the ability and the  responsibility to give back and it looks  different for every person  [Music]

